WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.247 --> 00:00:06.420
VARSHA DATTA: Hey, Everyone.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:09.330
My name is Varsha Datta, and
I'm a security and compliance

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.400
specialist with Google Cloud.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.960
And today I would
like to talk to you

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.120
about some of our
newest products

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.690
at Google reCAPTCHA Enterprise.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.660
Before we go into Google's
reCAPTCHA Enterprise product,

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.000
let's talk a little bit
about what a CAPTCHA is

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.155
and what it does.

00:00:25.155 --> 00:00:28.260
A CAPTCHA as an acronym
for completely automated

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.400
public Turing test to tell
computers and humans apart.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.210
It's a type of challenge
response test used in computing

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:39.600
to determine whether a
user is a human or not.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.490
Fairly straightforward, right?

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:42.630
Not really.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.420
There are a lot of nuances
that go into identifying

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.840
a real user versus a bot,
and reCAPTCHA Enterprise

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.840
is a complete solution
just build to do that

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.950
and do this very well.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:58.160
Let's talk a little bit about
some of these evolving threats

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.230
that we've seen in the industry.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:02.570
Users are constantly
under attack

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:05.600
from credential stuffing,
account takeovers,

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.550
and fraud all the time.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:11.270
And it's really unique compared
to protecting our own employees

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.160
within the company,
because we have

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.320
less control over
the environment

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.300
that our users are in.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.540
But they are as important
to our business.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.360
We've seen that, in
2018 alone, there

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.250
were about 20 billion attempted
logins with stolen credentials,

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:32.390
and 29% of those breaches
involved those stolen

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:33.950
credentials.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:38.510
And an estimated 8% of revenue
for many online businesses

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:41.160
is lost to fraud every year.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.980
The problem is in the
defense of these systems,

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:47.850
especially when you go all the
way to the users' computers,

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:50.480
because it requires a
lot of data and ability

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.540
to protect at the client
level in an environment

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.220
that you do not control.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.990
As you can imagine,
Google has a lot

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.900
of experience in dealing
with fraudulent activities

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.440
on our platform.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:05.520
We have over 1
billion users each

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.570
across all of these
nine different products,

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:11.940
and we only learn how to better
protect our users from fraud

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.070
over the last few years.

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.540
Just for some background,
here at Google

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.600
we've been protecting
our own systems

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:20.460
with two spectacular
technologies

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:21.810
over the last decade.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:25.170
The first is reCAPTCHA, which
defends more than 2 million

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:27.390
websites from fraudulent bots.

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:30.840
And the second one is
Safe Browsing technology,

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.810
which defends over
3 billion devices

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.790
from phishing and malware.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:38.550
Let's take a look at
these products in detail.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:42.030
Some of the more common
types of fraudulent attacks

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:45.690
we see are account takeovers,
synthetic accounts,

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:48.690
false posts, fraudulent
transactions, and money

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:50.790
laundering. reCAPTCHA
Enterprise is

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.590
built upon the existing
reCAPTCHA platform

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.530
that most of us
are familiar with.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.510
The goal of reCAPTCHA
Enterprise is

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.450
to defend against these
various threats, including,

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:05.100
but not limited to scraping,
fraud, and account takeovers.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.740
Now, we want to make it very
difficult for those attackers

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.430
to gain access to your
business to consumer services

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.980
and use them for
malicious purposes.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.260
Most importantly,
reCAPTCHA Enterprise--

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.760
with reCAPTCHA
enterprise, we can

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.610
do this while providing
a frictionless experience

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:23.730
to your users and customers.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.010
The product acts
as a barrier that

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.880
is placed in front of
your web applications,

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.460
where actions can be taken
to stop bots and fraudulent

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.500
activities from allow--

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:39.300
and while still
allowing your users in.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.830
Now, let's look at the
evolution of Google's reCAPTCHA

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:43.180
over the years.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.790
It got started with this
squiggly bit of text

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:47.980
that you're trying
to read and enter.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:53.180
And ask bots got better,
our defenses had to evolve.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:56.410
And that's why you see these
click boxes and crosswalks

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:59.640
where the challenges have become
a little bit more complex.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:02.590
And a lot of users actually
have trouble solving these,

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:06.170
and it's not the best
user experience either.

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:09.750
Not only do we face
threats from AI,

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:12.220
but we also face
threats from farming.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:14.340
A large number of
groups of people

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.070
are solving these
challenges for fraudsters,

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:21.070
and now it comes down to
actually distinguishing

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:23.170
the transactions from
fraudulent versus

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:27.890
non-fraudulent transactions,
and not just humans to bots.

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:30.010
The latest version of
reCAPTCHA Enterprise

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:31.960
is essentially a
piece of JavaScript

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:36.310
that interacts with the user
and gives you a risk score

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:39.070
to identify whether the
transaction or the user

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:42.550
interaction was alleged
to make one or not.

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:45.890
So this means no text
boxes and no crosswalks,

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:47.680
and just a snippet
of JavaScript that

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:49.810
sits within your HTML pages.

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:52.720
And there is no user interaction
required for the JavaScript

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.870
to start working, which
means no friction from having

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:58.470
to actually solve the challenge.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:02.040
It's also based on the
user's behavioral analysis

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.400
on your backside, rather
than relying on just one form

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:09.050
of signage on your webpage.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:11.330
So let's take a closer
look at what's included

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.200
within reCAPTCHA Enterprise.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.320
Now, if you're familiar
with reCAPTCHA v3,

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:17.610
the Enterprise version
includes a lot of--

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:19.940
all of v3 and a lot more.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:21.950
So it includes an
enhanced risk score

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:25.550
based mechanism which is
based on machine learning,

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:28.190
and is also more
specific to your web site

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:30.500
than just generating bots.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:33.530
We also provide risk
codes or reason codes

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:35.510
that allow you to
better understand

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:41.640
why we are detecting a certain
behavior as a bot or not.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.900
Now, as a customer, you
also have the ability

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:46.900
to use the annotation API
to provide us feedback

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:50.740
whether or not a
certain risk score

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:52.870
that we provided
was a false positive

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:54.520
versus a false negative.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:57.520
Now, remember that not
all bots are good or bad.

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:59.680
It really comes down to
understanding your user

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.020
behaviors and understanding
what your business needs are.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:05.880
Now, with reCAPTCHA
Enterprise, we also

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:11.460
have mobile support with mobile
SDKs for Android and iOS.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:14.710
We also have a new password
leak detection mechanism

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:17.170
that's in early access.

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:19.020
We also have a
two-factor authentication

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:22.290
that can be implemented
for potential fraudsters

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:26.040
to further assert their
identity on your website.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:29.280
And finally, since we are on
the Google Cloud platform,

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:35.022
we provide SLA and SLO, and
also the standard GCP terms.

00:06:35.022 --> 00:06:37.680
Now, since reCAPTCHA
Enterprise actually

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.930
looks at the user
behavior, and how

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:41.940
they interact with the
page and synchronously

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:44.310
in the background,
the reCAPTCHA servers

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:47.190
formulate a risk score
based on the anomalies

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:49.340
that we have found
on the website.

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:50.910
Now, with the
Enterprise version,

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.975
we are able to provide more
granularity in these risk

00:06:53.975 --> 00:06:56.250
scores that range
between 0 to 1--

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:59.910
0 being a bad actor, and
1 being a good actor.

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:03.000
Now, some bots actually
have a bit of a gray area

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:06.000
that we need to provide
more details about.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.710
And that's why the enterprise
version actually provides you

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:13.110
a 10 point score, which allows
our customers to take more

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:16.350
precise decisions
on their website

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:19.980
and how they want to apply these
protections on their website.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:22.410
Now, as an example,
you almost always

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:25.500
want to have a different level
of protection and security

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:27.930
around your high
value pages like log

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:31.920
in or check out,
rather than a homepage.

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:35.940
Now, let's take a closer look at
some of these risk identifiers

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:37.540
or reason codes.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:40.080
We're working on adding more
and more of these reason

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.863
codes every day, but
here are a few of them.

00:07:42.863 --> 00:07:44.280
Now, when a risk
score is returned

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:48.330
that matches any behaviors
in this list and more,

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:50.310
we are providing
additional context

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:52.740
in the form of reason
codes that can assist you

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:58.470
in tuning your responses to your
users and identifying trends.

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:00.650
Now let's do a
high level overview

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:03.860
of how all of these
parts work together.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:05.600
The page or the
pages are actually

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:07.610
embedded with the
reCAPTCHA Enterprise.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:09.950
Now you can either have
it on one single page

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:12.410
of your website, or
it could be across all

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:14.540
of your different
pages on the website.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:17.570
The JavaScript will gather
the necessary data required

00:08:17.570 --> 00:08:19.550
to process the risk score.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:23.330
And then that data is passed
along as a tokenized element

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:26.720
and encrypted to the end user.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:29.810
The predicted web application
will then receive that token

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:32.390
when the user actually
interacts with the website,

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:35.690
and then passes that token
along over to the reCAPTCHA

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:37.700
Enterprise server.

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:40.100
And then once the
reCAPTCHA Enterprise server

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:42.159
is able to collect
all of the parameters,

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:44.930
and analyze it, and
provide a risk score,

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:46.640
it actually sends
that risk score back

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:48.922
to your infrastructure.

00:08:48.922 --> 00:08:50.630
Now, based on the
score, you can actually

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:52.340
make a decision
whether or not you want

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:54.350
to allow your user to continue.

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:57.560
And the good news
for current v3 users

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.860
is that the implementation is
quite similar across reCAPTCHA

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:02.840
Enterprise and reCAPTCHA v3.

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:05.510
So if you're already a
v3 user, the transition

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:08.735
is pretty much just aligned to
point to a reCAPTCHA Enterprise

00:09:08.735 --> 00:09:09.235
endpoint.

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:14.950
So now that we have a risk
score, what kind of action

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:16.240
do we take?

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:17.740
If a high risk
score is returned,

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:20.060
it's more likely that
it's a real user.

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:22.900
And there's-- you wouldn't
want to allow them to continue

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:24.950
their journey on your website.

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:27.940
Now, if it's a low risk score,
depending on the recent code,

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:31.900
you might want to take either
a few types of actions.

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:35.230
You might want to either reject
that user entirely and block

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:37.360
them on your
website, or you might

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:39.130
want to provide a
further challenge,

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:40.660
like a two-factor
authentication,

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:45.430
to allow that user to further
assert their identity.

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:47.560
Either way, you
would want to log

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:52.920
all of these actions
for further analysis.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:55.060
Now let's look at
some success stories

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:56.870
around reCAPTCHA Enterprise.

00:09:56.870 --> 00:09:59.530
Spotify was one of
our earlier adopters

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:01.190
of reCAPTCHA Enterprise.

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:03.050
We all know Spotify,
and we love it.

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:05.210
They were disruptors
in the media industry

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:07.970
and transformed how
we listen to music.

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:10.280
Some of the challenges
that Spotify was facing

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:12.380
included detection
of bot traffic

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:15.080
and threats such as
credential stuffing,

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:18.020
and abuse on Spotify's
website and apps.

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:19.850
With reCAPTCHA
Enterprise, they were not

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:22.650
enable to effectively
detect bot traffic,

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:25.370
but also protect and
defend against threats

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:27.730
coming from these bots.

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:31.570
Another one-- another great
example is Empower Illinois.

00:10:31.570 --> 00:10:34.690
Empower Illinois is a
state-wide scholarship

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:38.590
granting organization that
was created in 2017 to serve

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:42.400
children of Illinois through the
state's tax credit scholarship

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:43.570
program.

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:45.700
Now, some of the challenges
that they were facing

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:48.730
was fraudulent applications
of machines and credential

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:51.090
stuffing attacks by bots.

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:52.600
With reCAPTCHA
Enterprise, they were

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:55.090
able to accurately
detect bots, and also

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:58.210
create a safe passage
for legitimate users,

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:02.128
and minimize the user
friction on their website.

00:11:02.128 --> 00:11:04.640
Now let's take a
look at Web Risk API.

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:07.040
Web Risk API is a
Google Cloud Service

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:10.220
that allows customer web
applications to check URLs

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:14.300
against Google's constantly
updated list of unsafe web

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:15.740
resources.

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:18.500
Web Risk API helps
defend against phishing

00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:21.620
links, malware
links, spam links,

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:23.870
amongst other malicious URLs.

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:27.290
It acts as a barrier between
fraudsters and customer website

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:31.130
applications, protecting them
from malicious URLs being

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:34.010
posted on their website.

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:37.780
Let's take a closer
look at Web Risk API.

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:41.910
We all know that URLs contain
unsafe elements like phishing,

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:43.930
malware, and unwanted software.

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:48.680
And it's often impossible to
find this before our users do.

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:52.870
That's why we have Web Risk API,
a new offering from GCP that

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:57.930
enables any company to find
unsafe URLs on their website.

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:02.410
Now, Web Risk includes data on
more than a million unsafe URLs

00:12:02.410 --> 00:12:03.550
and users--

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:06.790
and uses Google's Safe
Browsing technology

00:12:06.790 --> 00:12:11.110
to defend against
malicious URLs.

00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:15.060
Now let's take a look at how
web risk API actually works.

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:19.100
Assume that attackers are
trying to exploit the ACME

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:23.070
reviews by posting unsafe links
onto an even submission feature

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:24.680
page.

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:27.590
To defend their
users and the site

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:30.500
against these kind
of attacks, ACME

00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:34.230
decides to use the Web Risk API
to filter all incoming calls

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:36.660
for unsafe content.

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:38.630
ACME needs to
essentially extract

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:40.910
those URLs from the
post and send them

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:44.495
to Web Risk API, where--

00:12:44.495 --> 00:12:47.180
and Web Risk API will
be able to identify

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:49.760
whether a URL is unsafe or not.

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:53.395
Then depending on the decision
that Web Risk API makes,

00:12:53.395 --> 00:12:56.570
ACME can then decide
to either post or not

00:12:56.570 --> 00:12:59.980
post those URLs
on their website.

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:03.460
Now let's look into some
of these threat categories.

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:06.150
Web Risk API also
provides information

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:09.120
on why a certain URL
was deemed malicious

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:11.940
based on some of the mentioned
threat types on the screen

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:12.600
here.

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:16.530
Now, this allows customers to
take a more educated decision

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:20.920
on whether or not to block
these malicious URLs.

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:24.120
Now here's a
remediation response.

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:27.510
For example, let's take a
look at the sample workflow

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:29.470
for Web Risk API.

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:32.700
Once the API returns the
result, if the URL is not

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:34.590
a malicious one,
our customers can

00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:38.430
choose to allow the link to be
then utilized on their website.

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:42.450
But if the URL is malicious,
their customer then--

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:45.750
the customer can either
decide to block the access

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:49.860
to the website entirely by using
something like a web proxy,

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:52.740
or flag the post as
a malicious pose,

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:55.380
or even block the post
entirely from being

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:57.270
submitted on the website.

00:13:57.270 --> 00:13:59.220
Either way, logging
these activities

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:01.470
is always a good
practice to allow scope

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:04.720
for future analysis.

